# CommunicationObject

## Purpose

A CommunicationObject represents a KNX communication object.

It acts as the boundary between:

- the application
- the KNX bus

The object:

- stores a current value
- listens on one or more KNX group addresses
- optionally transmits values to the KNX bus
- optionally updates its internal state from KNX telegrams
- optionally forwards received values to the application

The behaviour is controlled through flags.

---

# State

A CommunicationObject contains:

- current value
- one or more group addresses
- a set of flags
- optional application callbacks

The first configured group address is the sending group address.

All configured group addresses are used for receiving telegrams.

---

# Application Interface

## Read current value

The application can read the current value.

This operation never generates KNX traffic.

---

## Set value

The application can assign a new value.

The assigned value is considered a locally generated value.

When the TRANSMIT flag is enabled, a GroupValueWrite telegram is sent to the sending group address.

When the TRANSMIT flag is disabled, no telegram is sent.

The local value already represents the new application value regardless of transmission.

---

# KNX Telegram Processing

The object may receive:

- GroupValueRead
- GroupValueWrite
- GroupValueResponse

for any configured group address.

---

# Flag Semantics

## COMMUNICATION

The object participates in KNX communication.

When disabled, all incoming telegrams are ignored and no outgoing telegrams are generated.

---

## READ

Defines whether the object responds to GroupValueRead requests.

### Enabled

Receiving a GroupValueRead causes the object to transmit a GroupValueResponse containing its current value.

### Disabled

Receiving a GroupValueRead has no effect.

---

## WRITE

Defines whether incoming values are forwarded to the application.

Incoming values are:

- GroupValueWrite
- GroupValueResponse

### Enabled

The application callback is invoked with the received value.

### Disabled

The application callback is not invoked.

---

## UPDATE

Defines whether incoming values modify the internal state.

Incoming values are:

- GroupValueWrite
- GroupValueResponse

### Enabled

The internal value becomes the received value.

### Disabled

The internal value remains unchanged.

---

## TRANSMIT

Defines whether locally generated values are transmitted to the KNX bus.

### Enabled

Calling set_value() generates a GroupValueWrite telegram.

### Disabled

Calling set_value() does not generate a telegram.

---

# Flag Combinations

## UPDATE only

Incoming KNX values update internal state.

The application is not notified.

Result:

Bus -> State

---

## WRITE only

Incoming KNX values are forwarded to the application.

Internal state is unchanged.

Result:

Bus -> Application

---

## UPDATE + WRITE

Incoming KNX values update internal state and notify the application.

Result:

Bus -> State
Bus -> Application

---

## TRANSMIT + WRITE + UPDATE

Locally set values are transmitted to the bus.

Received values update state and notify the application.

This is the typical configuration for a bidirectional KNX object.

---

# Multiple Group Addresses

A CommunicationObject may have multiple group addresses.

Example:

- 1/1/1
- 1/1/2

The first address is the sending group address.

Outgoing telegrams are always sent to the first address.

Incoming telegrams from any configured address are processed.

This allows a single object to use:

- a command address
- a status address

while sharing one internal value.

---

# Echo Behaviour

Some KNX gateways return transmitted telegrams back to the sender as incoming telegrams.

Such an echoed telegram is processed exactly like any other incoming telegram.

A CommunicationObject does not distinguish between:

- a telegram generated by another KNX participant
- an echoed version of its own telegram

Therefore application callbacks may be triggered again when echo is present.

The object relies entirely on flag behaviour and does not perform echo suppression.

---

# Conceptual Model

Application

    |
    v

CommunicationObject

    - Current Value
    - Flags
    - Group Addresses
    - Callbacks

    |
    v

KNX Bus

The application interacts only with the CommunicationObject.

The CommunicationObject is responsible for all KNX-specific behaviour including:

- read responses
- value updates
- callback invocation
- telegram transmission
- multi-address handling